[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 1. Uninitialised stack buffers were modelled as zeros
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin:  0 0 3 65 66 67 3 65 66 67        (operation 0, "ABC" vs "ABC", no NUL)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:      0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:   1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` declares
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m char input_buffer[MAX_BUFFER_SIZE];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m char ref_buffer[MAX_BUFFER_SIZE];
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and writes only `input_len` / `ref_len` bytes into them. It never appends a NUL.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `validate_token` then calls `strcmp(token, expected)`, which walks past those
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m three bytes into whatever the dynamic loader and libc start-up left on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stack. That residue is non-zero, so the two buffers differ at index 3 and the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns 0 (invalid).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust code zero-filled both buffers, which made them look NUL-terminated at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m index 3, so `strcmp` reported equality and the program printed 1. The same error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m affected every operation, because every helper in `lib.c` reads past the length
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it was given.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/uninit.rs` now carries the residue as data: two 1024-byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tables recovered from the reference binary. They were extracted by using the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program's own comparisons as oracles rather than by guessing:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * *Is `input_buffer[k]` a NUL?* Run operation 0 with `input_len = k` (filler
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes) and a fully specified 1024-byte reference holding the same `k` bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   followed by zeros. `strcmp` returns equal only when `input_buffer[k] == 0`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the printed 1/0 is a direct read of that one bit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * *What is `input_buffer[k]`?* Run operation 2 with `flags = 0`, which reduces
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   to `strncmp(input, ref, strlen(ref))`. With a reference of `k` filler bytes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   one candidate byte `c`, then zeros, the comparison length is `k + 1` and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   program prints 1 only when `input_buffer[k] == c`. Sweeping `c` over 1..255
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   yields the byte.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `ref_buffer` was read the same way, walking each run of non-zero bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   backwards from the NUL that ends it, since only the last unknown byte of a run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   can be isolated at a time.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The residue turned out to be mostly leftover 64-bit pointers, which is why the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *positions* of the NUL bytes are stable (the top two bytes of every pointer are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m zero) while some individual non-zero bytes change from run to run under ASLR.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Positions that could not be pinned down hold a non-zero placeholder, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m placeholder differs between the two tables so that comparing one garbage region
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m against the other fails the way it does in C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the single highest-impact fix: NUL placement is what determines `strlen`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `strlen` determines almost every value the C code returns for a partially
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m filled buffer.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 2. Reads past the end of a buffer stopped at the array instead of continuing into the frame
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin:  0 0 1024 <1024x 65> 1024 <1024x 65>
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:      0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:   1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and the same disagreement for operations 2 (both flag settings) and 4, plus:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin:  4 2 1024 <1024x 1> 1024 <1024x 1>
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:      killed by SIGSEGV
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:   1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m With `input_len == 1024` and no NUL among the bytes, `strlen(input_buffer)` runs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m off the end of the array itself. The Rust model treated anything past the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1024-byte slice as `\0`, which made both strings look like exactly 1024 `A`s and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m therefore equal.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `objdump -d c_src/build/driver` shows what really follows. `main` builds its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m frame with `sub $0x840,%rsp` and addresses its locals as:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x838   unsigned int byte      (reference read loop temp)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x834   unsigned int byte      (input read loop temp)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x830   char ref_buffer[1024]
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x430   char input_buffer[1024]
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x030   size_t ref_len
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x028   size_t input_len
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x020   (4 bytes of padding)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x01c   uint32_t flags
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x018   int operation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x014   int result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x010   size_t i               (reference read loop)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp-0x008   size_t i               (input read loop)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp+0x000   saved rbp
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rbp+0x008   return address
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `ref_buffer` sits **directly below** `input_buffer`; reading past the end of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the reference continues into the input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Reading past the end of `input_buffer` lands on `ref_len`, then `input_len`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m In the failing case `ref_len` is 1024 = `0x400`, whose first little-endian byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is `0x00`, so the C `strlen(input_buffer)` is 1024 - while
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strlen(ref_buffer)` is 2048, because it runs through all of `input_buffer`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first. The two strings have different lengths and `strcmp` returns non-zero.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/frame.rs` models `main`'s whole frame as one flat byte array
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with `ref_buffer` at offset 0, `input_buffer` at 1024 and the scalar locals
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m after it, and `translation/src/strcpy_fun.rs` was rewritten so that a C `char *`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is an *index* into that array rather than a Rust slice. `strlen`, `strcmp` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strncmp` therefore walk out of one buffer and into the next exactly as they do
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in the reference program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Because `main` has already rejected any length above 1024, the top six bytes of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ref_len` are always zero, so an unterminated `input_buffer` always finds a NUL
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m within two bytes of the array end. That is what makes this whole class of input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deterministic and testable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 3. `strtoul` saturation was applied after the sign instead of before it
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdin:  0 0 -9999999999999999999999999999999999999999 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C:      stderr "Error: input length 18446744073709551615 exceeds maximum 1024", exit 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust:   stderr "Error reading reference length", exit 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%zu", ...)` converts with `strtoul`. When the digits overflow,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtoul` returns `ULONG_MAX` regardless of any leading minus sign - the sign is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m only applied to a magnitude that actually fitted. The Rust reader clamped the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m magnitude to `u64::MAX` and *then* negated it, producing `input_len == 1`. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program went on to read one byte and a reference length, so it failed several
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tokens later with a different message.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/src/scan.rs` now tracks overflow as a separate flag:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtoul` yields `u64::MAX` whenever the magnitude overflowed, and negates only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m otherwise. `%d` follows the matching `strtol` rule (saturate at `LONG_MIN` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MAX`, then truncate into an `int`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. Trailing-newline and flush behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No mismatch was found here, but it was checked explicitly since it is easy to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m get wrong: `printf("%d\n", result)` is the only thing the C ever writes to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout, all error paths write to stderr and return 1, and no path writes to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both. The Rust program prints with a trailing `\n` and flushes before returning,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `ExitCode::from(1)` reproduces `return 1` from `main`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Inputs the reference program cannot be compared on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Some inputs make the C code read residue bytes that ASLR randomises, so the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program has no single answer. These are genuinely non-deterministic and no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation can match them; measured over 800 runs each:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | C output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0 0 2 79 75 3 122 122 122` (unterminated `"OK"`) | `0` or `1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    202[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `1 0 4 83 84 79 80 0` (unterminated `"STOP"`) | `-1` or `1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    203[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `3 0 4 78 79 78 69 1 124` (unterminated `"NONE"`, `'\|'`) | `-1` or `-2` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    204[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2 0 6 65 66 67 68 69 70 3 65 66 67` (both unterminated) | `0` or `1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    205[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0 0 1 0 0` (`ref_len == 0`, so `strlen(ref_buffer)` reads residue) | `0` or `1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    206[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    207[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The pattern is the same in each: the result turns on a residue byte that holds a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    208[0m:[38;5;10m[49m randomised middle byte of a leftover pointer. `ref_buffer[0]` is zero in roughly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    209[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6% of runs, which is what makes any `ref_len == 0` input that calls
