[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation compiled cleanly but was wrong in three ways, all found by running both binaries against each other.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m1. Uninitialised stack buffers were modelled as zeros.[22m [38;5;10mmain[0m writes only [38;5;10minput_len[0m bytes into [38;5;10mchar input_buffer[1024][0m and never NUL-terminates it, while every helper in [38;5;10mlib.c[0m calls [38;5;10mstrcmp[0m/[38;5;10mstrlen[0m on it. Zero-filling made the buffers look terminated, so [38;5;10m0 0 3 65 66 67 3 65 66 67[0m printed [38;5;10m1[0m where the C prints [38;5;10m0[0m. I recovered the real loader residue (two 1024-byte tables) by using the program's own comparisons as bit oracles — operation 0 answers "is [38;5;10minput_buffer[k][0m NUL?", operation 2 with [38;5;10mflags=0[0m answers "is [38;5;10minput_buffer[k] == c[0m?".[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m2. Overruns stopped at the array instead of continuing into the frame.[22m With a full 1024-byte buffer, [38;5;10mstrlen[0m leaves the array entirely. [38;5;10mobjdump[0m on the reference binary shows [38;5;10mref_buffer[0m sits directly below [38;5;10minput_buffer[0m, and past [38;5;10minput_buffer[0m come [38;5;10mref_len[0m then [38;5;10minput_len[0m. So [38;5;10mstrlen(ref_buffer)[0m is 2048 for a full buffer while [38;5;10mstrlen(input_buffer)[0m is 1024 — the C returns "not equal" where Rust returned "equal", and one case that segfaults in C returned [38;5;10m1[0m. I replaced the slice-based model with an explicit model of [38;5;10mmain[0m's whole stack frame, where a [38;5;10mchar *[0m is an index, so reads walk from one buffer into the next.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1m3. [38;5;10mstrtoul[0m saturation was applied after the sign.[22m For [38;5;10m-9999…[0m (40 digits), glibc returns [38;5;10mULONG_MAX[0m regardless of the minus sign; Rust clamped then negated, giving [38;5;10minput_len == 1[0m and a different error message several tokens later.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## Testing[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mtranslation/tests/differential.rs[0m has 27 tests over 275 distinct inputs, spawning both binaries and comparing stdout, stderr, exit code and terminating signal. Coverage is enumerated from the C: every [38;5;10mscanf[0m failure point with its indexed message, both length bounds, [38;5;10mstrtol[0m/[38;5;10mstrtoul[0m saturation, [38;5;10m(char)[0m truncation, all six [38;5;10moperation[0m branches, all five commands plus [38;5;10mADMIN[0m, all five [38;5;10mcompare_prefix[0m variations with the 63-byte [38;5;10mstrncpy[0m/[38;5;10mstrncat[0m boundary, both [38;5;10mfind_delimiter[0m special cases, all three [38;5;10msnprintf[0m wildcard forms with their 63-byte truncation, and the unsigned-wraparound scan that kills the C with [38;5;10mSIGSEGV[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One thing worth flagging: the C program is genuinely non-deterministic on a small set of inputs, because some residue bytes are randomised middle bytes of leftover pointers ([38;5;10mref_buffer[0][0m is zero in about 6% of runs). Those inputs have no single answer to match, so [38;5;10massert_same[0m re-runs the reference three times and fails loudly rather than flaking, and the suite is restricted to inputs I verified stable — each of the 275 was run against the C 400 times, 110,000 runs, byte-identical throughout. The five unusable inputs are tabulated in [38;5;10mERRORS.md[0m. A further ~8,000 random inputs agreed outside that set.[0m[0m
